6: <!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n327: ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n328: - Dimension: none remaining; synthesis\n329: - Focus area: the compiled review report, the planning packet and the final verdict.\n330: - Reason: all four dimensions are covered; one active P1 and seven P2s are recorded.\n331: - Rotation status: correctness, security, traceability and maintainability complete (CONDITIONAL).\n332: - Blocked/productive carry-forward: no blocked approach; the four-iteration trail is complete.\n333: - Required evidence: the registry's active counts, the iteration final lines and the synthesis report.\n334: \n335: <!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n336: \n337: ---\n338: \n339: ## 13. KNOWN CONTEXT\n340: \n341: ### Bounded Context Snapshot\n342: \n343: - Target pointers: `runtime/lib/command-attach.ts`, `runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`,\n344:   `runtime/lib/scorer/doctor-helper-handoff.ts`, `runtime/lib/scorer/projection.ts`,\n345:   `runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`, and the six changed test files.\n346: - Behavior claims: phase 020 spec `REQ-003`, `REQ-004`, `REQ-006`, `REQ-007`; phase 024's mention\n347:   reader decisions P022 and P024; phase 021's head-row parity decision P015.\n348: - Reuse and conventions: the generated command tables are outputs of\n349:   `runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs`; the `--check` run is fresh.\n350:   Review doctrine is `sk-code-review/references/review-core.md` §2.\n351: - Review risks and gaps: the local compiled dist is stale (system reminder). Tests read source\n352:   through vitest, so a source-level probe is valid, but a live runtime could still load dist.\n353:   No live per-runtime session is available; cross-runtime claims rest on the runtime-neutral\n354:   handler and the shared tests.\n355: - Out of scope: transcript scorer arithmetic, renderer output, holdout prompt text.\n356: \n357: ---\n358: \n359: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-reference-status -->\n360: ## 14. CROSS-REFERENCE STATUS\n361: \n362: | Protocol | Level | Status | Iteration | Notes |\n363: |----------|-------|--------|-----------|-------|\n364: | `spec_code` | core | partial | 3 | REQ-003/004 exact prompts pass; REQ-007 says 10 while AC-007 and the code use 9; the coordinated refusal is an uncovered class (SA4-P1-002). |\n365: | `checklist_evidence` | core | partial | 3 | AC-002/003/004/006/007 are Met with evidence; AC-007 records 9 while REQ-007 says 10; CHK-FIX-004's \"two commands\" case is a request, not a coordinated refusal. |\n366: | `skill_agent` | overlay | notApplicable | 3 | No agent definition changed in this area. |\n367: | `agent_cross_runtime` | overlay | partial | 3 | No runtime branch in the changed attach/handler files; live per-runtime proof belongs to the phase 007 arm. |\n368: | `feature_catalog_code` | overlay | partial | 3 | `advisor-recommend.md:39` stale after the head-row change; `command-meaning-matching.md:65` stale count; doctor routing text still true. |\n369: | `playbook_capability` | overlay | notApplicable | - | No playbook surface in the changed files |\n370: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-reference-status -->","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/scorer-attach-4/deep-review-strategy.md, lines 1-370\n1: ---\n2: title: Deep Review Strategy - scorer-attach-4 lineage\n3: description: Runtime strategy tracking review progress for the scorer-attach fan-out lineage, fourth pass of command surfacing.\n4: version: 1.11.14.0\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # Deep Review Strategy - scorer-attach-4 lineage\n8: \n9: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n10: \n11: ### Purpose\n12: \n13: Persistent brain for this fan-out lineage. It records which dimensions remain, what was found\n14: (P0/P1/P2), which review approaches worked or failed, and where to focus next. Read at every\n15: iteration before any review action.\n16: \n17: ### Ownership\n18: \n19: Machine-managed metrics and coverage blocks are wrapped in explicit ownership markers. Human\n20: commentary and operator overrides live outside those markers.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. TOPIC\n25: \n26: Review pass four of `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build`\n27: in the `scorer-attach` area. Scope: the eleven changed paths listed in\n28: `review/review-scope-4.txt` whose area is `scorer-attach` — the attach module, the recommend\n29: handler, the scorer modules that gate command rows, and their tests — plus shared docs that\n30: describe them. The change range is `0cdc9fa3ef..400c90e158` (HEAD at dispatch).\n31: \n32: The operator focus is the pass-four brief: checks on claude, codex, opencode, pi, devin and dsh;\n33: a refusal, question or bare mention of a command still attaching or starting a mutating command;\n34: the transcript scorer's load/followed/offer arithmetic; holdout hygiene; and docs that disagree\n35: with code.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: <!-- ANCHOR:review-dimensions -->\n40: ## 3. REVIEW DIMENSIONS (remaining)\n41: [All dimensions complete]\n42: \n43: <!-- /ANCHOR:review-dimensions -->\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 4. NON-GOALS\n48: \n49: - The renderer, hooks, plugin, session readers and doctor command docs are the\n50:   `render-hooks-plugin-4` and `doctor-docs-4` lineages' scope. This lineage reads them only as\n51:   context evidence.\n52: - The transcript scorer, command-offer harness, capture baseline and corpus files are the\n53:   `accuracy-runner-4` lineage's scope. The operator focus asks about their arithmetic; this\n54:   lineage records the boundary and does not raise code findings outside it.\n55: - Holdout prompt text is never read or printed. Corpus work is limited to counts and\n56:   `jq 'del(.prompt)'` rows.\n57: - No code changes: review is observation-only.\n58: \n59: ---\n60: \n61: ## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\n62: \n63: - Convergence: `newFindingsRatio` at or below the 0.10 threshold with no active P0.\n64: - Cap: `maxIterations` 4.\n65: - Stuck: two consecutive iterations with no new evidence (stuckThreshold 2).\n66: - Any active P0 blocks PASS regardless of convergence.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: <!-- ANCHOR:completed-dimensions -->\n71: ## 4. COMPLETED DIMENSIONS\n72: - [x] correctness\n73: - [x] security\n74: - [x] traceability\n75: - [x] maintainability\n76: \n77: <!-- /ANCHOR:completed-dimensions -->\n78: \n79: ---\n80: \n81: <!-- ANCHOR:running-findings -->\n82: ## 5. RUNNING FINDINGS\n83: - P0 (Blockers): 0\n84: - P1 (Required): 1\n85: - P2 (Suggestions): 7\n86: - Resolved: 0\n87: \n88: <!-- /ANCHOR:running-findings -->\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## 8. WHAT WORKED\n93: \n94: - Reading the three guards as one ordering chain instead of three independent checks: the retired-name gap only appears because `typedCommandIn` rewrites before `commandOnlyMentioned` reads, and the coordinated-refusal gap only appears because the lead must sit directly before each occurrence (iteration 1).\n95: - Probing the handler's real attach path: `scoreAdvisorPrompt` put `sk-doc` and `system-deep-loop` first for the two refusal prompts, and `typedCommandForTop` returned the command for both, so the defect was proven at the public recommendation boundary rather than only at the helper (iteration 1).\n96: - Running the compiled-route front door for the same prompts: it returned `action: defer`, which narrowed the live path to the typed attach and kept the finding honest about its caller (iteration 1).\n97: - Running the six changed test files first: 191 passed, so the new tests are green and the gaps are uncovered inputs rather than a failing suite (iteration 1).\n98: - Comparing the direct `what does /doctor:update do?` case with the `Is the /doctor:update command safe?` case: the first lost its command row and the second kept it, which isolated the missing `the ... command` gap in the bare-ask lead instead of blaming the doctor row (iteration 2).\n99: - Reading `publicRecommendation` before judging the probe: `scoreAdvisorPrompt` reports `command: null` on a command row, but the handler copies the generated row's command at `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:358-362`, so the public offer is real (iteration 2).\n100: - Reading the packet's accepted limits before keeping a P1: phase 020 T017 records the retired-alias refusal as a known limit, which moved that finding to P2 and kept the active set honest (iteration 3).\n101: - Comparing a requirement row with its acceptance row: `REQ-007` says the weak-cue bar is 10 and `AC-007` records 9, which located the stale side in the spec rather than asking the code to move (iteration 3).\n102: - Checking the generated bridge table with the deriver's `--check` before reporting doc drift: `status: fresh`, `changed: []`, so the projection is not hand-edited (iteration 3).\n103: - Treating each residual prompt as a would-be table row: the coordinated refusal, the `make sure` correction and the `Is the X command` question each fail at the assertion line, which proves the test gap without changing the suite (iteration 4).\n104: - Reading `CHK-FIX-004` against the table it cites: the \"two commands\" row is the request `use /speckit:plan, not /speckit:complete`, so the checklist claim is broader than the fence (iteration 4).\n105: \n106: ---\n107: \n108: ## 9. WHAT FAILED\n109: \n110: - Reading the mention reader as a complete refusal guard failed: its documented \"every occurrence has to carry a lead\" rule is what lets a coordinated refusal's second name through, so the reading produced finding `SA4-P1-002` instead of a clean pass (iteration 1).\n111: - Reading the small-edit gate's creation guard as a document-creation check failed: `make sure`, `add a` and `new` are ordinary correction clauses, and the gate stands down on all three, so the reading produced `SA4-P1-003` (iteration 1).\n112: - Reading the mention reader as covering every question form failed: the `Is the X command safe?` shape puts the noun between the ask word and the name, so the command row survived; it became `SA4-P2-001` rather than a clean security pass (iteration 2).\n113: - Reading `advisor-recommend.md:39` as still true failed: the changed head-row condition and its test prove the head keeps a command the partner row also carries, so the doc sentence is stale (iteration 3).\n114: - Reading the meaning-anchor doc's 17-case inventory as a subset failed: the new withhold cases are in the same file, and the whole file now holds 28 `it(` declarations (iteration 3).\n115: - Reading the changed test tables as a fence for the four residual behaviors failed: none of the four inputs has a row, so the suite stays green while they are live; it became `SA4-P2-005` (iteration 4).\n116: \n117: ---\n118: \n119: <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n120: ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n121: ### **`commandOnlyMentioned` can read a longer command as the shorter one.** Ruled out by `(?![a-z0-9_-])` and the changed test for `/create:sk-skill-parent`; the guard returns false for the shorter name. -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n122: - What was tried: **`commandOnlyMentioned` can read a longer command as the shorter one.** Ruled out by `(?![a-z0-9_-])` and the changed test for `/create:sk-skill-parent`; the guard returns false for the shorter name.\n123: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n124: - Do NOT retry: **`commandOnlyMentioned` can read a longer command as the shorter one.** Ruled out by `(?![a-z0-9_-])` and the changed test for `/create:sk-skill-parent`; the guard returns false for the shorter name.\n125: \n126: ### **A read-only command is withheld by the scorer on a no-edit request.** Ruled out: `withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit` returns the `command-doctor-env` row unchanged and the changed test asserts it. -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n127: - What was tried: **A read-only command is withheld by the scorer on a no-edit request.** Ruled out: `withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit` returns the `command-doctor-env` row unchanged and the changed test asserts it.\n128: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n129: - Do NOT retry: **A read-only command is withheld by the scorer on a no-edit request.** Ruled out: `withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit` returns the `command-doctor-env` row unchanged and the changed test asserts it.\n130: \n131: ### **Holdout rows are read by a changed source path.** Ruled out: no changed source file references either corpus file; the only corpus readers are tests that select tune ids and assert the split. -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n132: - What was tried: **Holdout rows are read by a changed source path.** Ruled out: no changed source file references either corpus file; the only corpus readers are tests that select tune ids and assert the split.\n133: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n134: - Do NOT retry: **Holdout rows are read by a changed source path.** Ruled out: no changed source file references either corpus file; the only corpus readers are tests that select tune ids and assert the split.\n135: \n136: ### **The `advisor-recommend.md:39` sentence is still true because the head and the row are different recommendations.** Ruled out: the changed test expects the head's `command` to equal the row's command and still finds the row, so the same command takes two slots. -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n137: - What was tried: **The `advisor-recommend.md:39` sentence is still true because the head and the row are different recommendations.** Ruled out: the changed test expects the head's `command` to equal the row's command and still finds the row, so the same command takes two slots.\n138: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n139: - Do NOT retry: **The `advisor-recommend.md:39` sentence is still true because the head and the row are different recommendations.** Ruled out: the changed test expects the head's `command` to equal the row's command and still finds the row, so the same command takes two slots.\n140: \n141: ### **The `REFUSE8` table's name is the defect.** Ruled out: the name encodes eight rows but the table is data-driven; the missing class, not the name, is the finding. -- BLOCKED (iteration 4, 1 attempts)\n142: - What was tried: **The `REFUSE8` table's name is the defect.** Ruled out: the name encodes eight rows but the table is data-driven; the missing class, not the name, is the finding.\n143: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n144: - Do NOT retry: **The `REFUSE8` table's name is the defect.** Ruled out: the name encodes eight rows but the table is data-driven; the missing class, not the name, is the finding.\n145: \n146: ### **The compiled route can attach the refused command.** Ruled out for the exact probe prompts: `compiled-route.cjs` returns `action: defer` for `do not run /create:skill` and for `do not run /deep:review or /deep:research`. The live attach is the typed path. -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n147: - What was tried: **The compiled route can attach the refused command.** Ruled out for the exact probe prompts: `compiled-route.cjs` returns `action: defer` for `do not run /create:skill` and for `do not run /deep:review or /deep:research`. The live attach is the typed path.\n148: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n149: - Do NOT retry: **The compiled route can attach the refused command.** Ruled out for the exact probe prompts: `compiled-route.cjs` returns `action: defer` for `do not run /create:skill` and for `do not run /deep:review or /deep:research`. The live attach is the typed path.\n150: \n151: ### **The doctor command row can be offered on a direct `what does` question.** Ruled out by the probe: `what does /doctor:update do?` passes only `system-skilled-doctor`; the command row is withheld. -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n152: - What was tried: **The doctor command row can be offered on a direct `what does` question.** Ruled out by the probe: `what does /doctor:update do?` passes only `system-skilled-doctor`; the command row is withheld.\n153: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n154: - Do NOT retry: **The doctor command row can be offered on a direct `what does` question.** Ruled out by the probe: `what does /doctor:update do?` passes only `system-skilled-doctor`; the command row is withheld.\n155: \n156: ### **The doctor question is covered by the direct `what does` case.** Ruled out: the direct case passes and withholds the row; the noun form does not. -- BLOCKED (iteration 4, 1 attempts)\n157: - What was tried: **The doctor question is covered by the direct `what does` case.** Ruled out: the direct case passes and withholds the row; the noun form does not.\n158: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n159: - Do NOT retry: **The doctor question is covered by the direct `what does` case.** Ruled out: the direct case passes and withholds the row; the noun form does not.\n160: \n161: ### **The generated projection was hand-edited.** Ruled out by the deriver's `--check`: `status: fresh`, `changed: []`. -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n162: - What was tried: **The generated projection was hand-edited.** Ruled out by the deriver's `--check`: `status: fresh`, `changed: []`.\n163: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n164: - Do NOT retry: **The generated projection was hand-edited.** Ruled out by the deriver's `--check`: `status: fresh`, `changed: []`.\n165: \n166: ### **The mention reader's 17-case inventory is an intentional subset.** Ruled out by the sentence's own list (cosine, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility, ranking), which cannot include the six new withhold cases. -- BLOCKED (iteration 3, 1 attempts)\n167: - What was tried: **The mention reader's 17-case inventory is an intentional subset.** Ruled out by the sentence's own list (cosine, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility, ranking), which cannot include the six new withhold cases.\n168: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n169: - Do NOT retry: **The mention reader's 17-case inventory is an intentional subset.** Ruled out by the sentence's own list (cosine, margin, stand-in refusal, eligibility, ranking), which cannot include the six new withhold cases.\n170: \n171: ### **The question case can silently write.** Ruled out for this command: `commandRowFor('command-doctor-update')?.policy` is `ask` (generated row, `command-attach.ts:178`), so the command asks before it acts. -- BLOCKED (iteration 2, 1 attempts)\n172: - What was tried: **The question case can silently write.** Ruled out for this command: `commandRowFor('command-doctor-update')?.policy` is `ask` (generated row, `command-attach.ts:178`), so the command asks before it acts.\n173: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n174: - Do NOT retry: **The question case can silently write.** Ruled out for this command: `commandRowFor('command-doctor-update')?.policy` is `ask` (generated row, `command-attach.ts:178`), so the command asks before it acts.\n175: \n176: ### **The retired-name rewrite itself is wrong.** Ruled out: `typedCommandIn('/create:skill')` returning `/create:sk-skill` is the phase's stated behavior, and the changed test pins it. The finding is the guard ordering, not the mapping. -- BLOCKED (iteration 1, 1 attempts)\n177: - What was tried: **The retired-name rewrite itself is wrong.** Ruled out: `typedCommandIn('/create:skill')` returning `/create:sk-skill` is the phase's stated behavior, and the changed test pins it. The finding is the guard ordering, not the mapping.\n178: - Why blocked: Repeated iteration evidence ruled this direction out.\n179: - Do NOT retry: **The retired-name rewrite itself is wrong.** Ruled out: `typedCommandIn('/create:skill')` returning `/create:sk-skill` is the phase