{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760793192,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0145b82001g2i2skDw19fvES","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","messageID":"msg_1e0144b18001iofEaiyAkSpnJj","type":"tool","id":"call_dc155306a51546668271888e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown"},"output":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 1-272\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\n3: description: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"doctor command scripts\"\n6:   - \"doctor route validation\"\n7:   - \"mcp diagnostics\"\n8: importance_tier: \"important\"\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Doctor Command Scripts\n12: \n13: > Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n14: > OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n15: >\n16: > Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n21: \n22: `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n23: \n24: The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n29: \n30: ```text\n31: .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n32: +-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n33: +-- agents-md-check.cjs\n34: +-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n35: +-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n36: +-- audit_descriptions.py\n37: +-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n38: +-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n39: +-- ci-report.cjs\n40: +-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n41: +-- ci-toggle.cjs\n42: +-- codex-hook-approval-check.cjs\n43: +-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n44: +-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n45: +-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n46: +-- git-setup-check.cjs\n47: +-- git-standards.cjs\n48: +-- hook-wiring-check.cjs\n49: +-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n50: +-- mcp-doctor.sh\n51: +-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n52: +-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n53: +-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n54: +-- route-validate.py\n55: +-- route-validate.sh\n56: +-- release-update.cjs\n57: +-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n58: +-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n59: +-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n60: +-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n61: +-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n62: +-- lib/\n63: |   +-- agents-md-sections.cjs      # the one segmenter, classifier, block reader and delivery measurement the two engines and their suites share\n64: |   +-- ci-profile.cjs             # the repository CI profile loader the report, scaffold and toggle scripts share\n65: |   +-- ci-report-render.cjs       # the report document, its JSON form and its fixed-width table\n66: |   +-- ci-report-sources.cjs      # repository variables, the branch runs come from and the newest non-rebuild commit\n67: |   +-- ci-report-text.cjs         # the one-line rule a gh failure and a YAML parse error both need\n68: |   +-- ci-report-workflows.cjs    # workflow discovery, the YAML parse, switch states and drift flags\n69: |   +-- quality-applied-rules.cjs  # the checklists and override rows the gate would apply, asked through --explain\n70: |   +-- quality-config-init.cjs    # the one mode that writes, from the shipped template with --apply\n71: |   +-- quality-config-source.cjs  # the config source, its trust state and the hygiene rules it switches off\n72: |   +-- quality-report-view.cjs    # the read-only status and check modes\n73: |   +-- quality-script-io.cjs      # the request error and the one place a report reaches stdout\n74: |   +-- quality-skill-paths.cjs    # the one sk-code quality folder and the handles every mode reads\n75: |   +-- sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs # the pure frontmatter, router-move and legacy-router text transforms\n76: |   `-- sk-skill-surface.cjs       # shared probes the check engine and the converter both call: locate, classify, collisions, spawn\n77: +-- tests/            # one suite per script, plus run-all.sh\n78: |   `-- opencode-version-battery.test.cjs\n79: `-- README.markdown\n80: ```\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 3. KEY FILES\n85: \n86: | File | Responsibility |\n87: |---|---|\n88: | `route-validate.sh` | Runs route-manifest validation and fixture-based self-tests. |\n89: | `route-validate.py` | Implements route schema, asset, tool, mutation and display-parity checks. |\n90: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnoses supported MCP servers and runtime configuration wiring. |\n91: | `mcp-doctor-lib.sh` | Supplies logging, result tracking, JSON output and configuration helpers to `mcp-doctor.sh`. |\n92: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Enforces read-only and mutating classifications for MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n93: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Audits parent skill hubs against structural and routing invariants. |\n94: | `release-update.cjs` | Plans, aligns, applies, rolls back and records framework release bases for the five `/doctor:update` workflows. |\n95: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n96: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n97: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n98: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n99: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n100: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n101: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n102: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Four of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block, the repo-rules checker's summary and the manifest generator's check of the source template's purity. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n103: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing or one appeared outside the block that no shipped section carries. It refuses a symlinked `REPO RULES.md` or Cursor mirror it would write, because a write through the link would change a file the repository does not own. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. It exits 3 when a write fails after the backup, having restored from it or said why not. |\n104: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n105: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n106: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n107: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n108: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n109: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n110: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n111: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n112: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.devin/hooks.v1.json` and `.vibe/hooks.toml`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is wired for each runtime folder it finds. An entry counts as wired only when one of its commands, read the way a shell reads it, starts node on that runtime's hook script, so a script named in an `echo` or a comment does not count. It reads `.vibe/hooks.toml` line by line for a `pre_agent` entry that runs the Vibe turn-start hook, because the repository installs no TOML parser. Pi, DSH and OpenCode load their prompt hook through an extension or a plugin, so the check has no row for them. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n113: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n114: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n115: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n116: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n117: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n118: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n119: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n120: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n121: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n122: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n123: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n124: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n125: | `lib/quality-config-init.cjs` | The one mode that can write: it shows the config it would create from the shipped template and writes it only with `--apply`, through a hard link from a temporary file, so it never replaces a config that already exists. An existing config is refused rather than merged. |\n126: | `lib/quality-report-view.cjs` | The two read-only modes. `status` reports the config, its trust, the tools it names, the hygiene layers and what the gate would apply here, and `check` stops at the config itself. Both render the lines a route prints and the document `--json` carries from one set of readings, and neither starts a configured check. |\n127: | `lib/quality-applied-rules.cjs` | Which checklists and hygiene rules the gate would apply to a repository and which override rows inside them it would honour, asked of the runner itself through `--explain` over a sampled file per folder group and extension, so the doctor keeps no second copy of checklist discovery or of the override grammar. |\n128: | `lib/sk-skill-surface.cjs` | The probes `/doctor:sk-skill` shares between its check engine and its converter: resolve a name or a path to a skill root, classify where that root sits (a direct child of `.skilled/skills`, outside it or nested in a hub), read the frontmatter fields, inspect name collisions against the reserved and persona prefixes, and wrap a spawned validator so its JSON, its exit status and its missing-tool cases read the same in both scripts. |\n129: | `lib/sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs` | The pure text transforms the converter applies, kept free of the repository so they are unit-tested on strings: normalize the `version` and `allowed-tools` frontmatter, find the machine blocks a Smart Routing section still holds, move a legacy trailing machine router section into the sibling router template, and add the router link line. |\n130: | `orphan-process-check.cjs` | Lists the helper processes and model servers this user started that were reparented to init and are older than a minimum age (60 minutes by default), classifying each as a node test, vitest, tail monitor, grep search, executor worker, session-handover test helper, model server or git-live-follow, and reports each one's age and memory with a total. `--reap` sends `SIGTERM` after re-checking each process's identity, `--yes` confirms it, and `--json` and `--summary` print machine-readable and one-line forms. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 on an unexpected error and 2 on a bad argument. |\n131: \n132: ---\n133: \n134: ## 4. ROUTE VALIDATION\n135: \n136: `route-validate.sh` delegates the live manifest checks to `route-validate.py`.\n137: \n138: The validator checks:\n139: \n140: - Manifest parsing and schema version\n141: - Required route fields\n142: - Unique targets\n143: - Referenced YAML assets\n144: - Mutation classes\n145: - MCP tool grants\n146: - Trigger phrases\n147: - Script path resolution\n148: - Each route names an existing doctor router\n149: - Target parity between each command's router and presentation\n150: - Read-only route mutation policy\n151: - Workflow activity coverage for every route script invocation\n152: \n153: Run it from the repository root:\n154: \n155: ```bash\n156: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n157: ```\n158: \n159: Run its negative fixture suite:\n160: \n161: ```bash\n162: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh --self-test\n163: ```\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. DIAGNOSTIC ENTRYPOINTS\n168: \n169: | Command | Purpose |\n170: |---|---|\n171: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--json]` | Report the twelve AGENTS.md rows for this repository or another one, and write nothing. |\n172: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs plan [--root <dir>] [--keep-claude-md] [--create-router]` | Print every target with its diff, the new block, the budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest. Writes nothing, and prints `NOTHING TO DO` when the repository is already in its target state. |\n173: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs apply --plan-digest <digest> [--root <dir>]` | Back up, write inside the allowlist and prove the composition, only with the digest the plan printed. |\n174: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs restore --run=<dir> --yes` | Put back exactly the paths one run recorded, and remove the files, and the empty folders, that run created. |\n175: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-manifest.cjs --check` | Fail when the manifest's derived key no longer matches the shipped documents. |\n176: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnose all supported MCP servers. |\n177: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh --json` | Emit machine-readable MCP diagnostics. |\n178: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs <skill-dir>` | Audit one parent skill hub. |\n179: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Report skill graph drift. |\n180: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-check.cjs check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | Report how far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format row by row, or report a skill's gaps against a hub's eleven surfaces. |\n181: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan <name-or-path>` | Print the conversion plan for one skill and write nothing. A plain skill refuses until at least eight `--approved-phrase` values are passed, and a skill with no `allowed-tools` field prints the list its plan proposes. |\n182: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply <name-or-path> --apply` | Write a plan already shown and approved: normalize the frontmatter and move a legacy machine router into `ROUTER.md`, or stage a plain skill as a new root. Without `--apply` it prints the plan and stops. `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves the reshaped body first and writes nothing. |\n183: | `python3 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/audit_descriptions.py --repo-root .` | Audit description budgets. |\n184: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Report agent-roster coverage drift across runtimes. |\n185: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Report command-catalog and hub-metadata drift. |\n186: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Check the read-only and mutating classes of MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n187: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/release-update.cjs check --offline` | Report what a release update would change, without fetching. |\n188: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-hook-gates.cjs list` | Show every hook gate and its saved setting. |\n189: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-standards.cjs status` | Show which commit, PR and branch rules are enforced, and from where. |\n190: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs` | Check which sk-git hooks, rules and settings are live in this repository. |\n191: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-code-quality-config.cjs status` | Show which quality config the gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, the trust state the runner applies to that config, the hygiene rules it switches off and the checklists and override rows the gate would apply. |\n192: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Check this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. |\n193: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\\|v2\\|both` | Choose the family to check. The default is `both`. |\n194: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --json` | Print the JSON summary on stdout instead of the human lines. |\n195: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --root <dir> --out <file>` | Inspect another repository root and write the JSON summary to the named file. |\n196: \n197: The battery runs five legs:\n198: \n199: - `family-present`: PASS when a candidate's `--version` succeeds and parses to that family.\n200: - `plugin-discovered`: PASS when OpenCode 1's `debug config` exits 0 with parseable JSON, the plugin folder holds at least one top-level `.js` file, none is a broken symlink and every one is listed.\n201: - `plugin-load`: PASS when the import check exits 0 and reports every top-level `.js` file loaded.\n202: - `plugin-active`: PASS when two consecutive reads give the same non-zero count and, on that read, every top-level `.js` file has an entry under the inspected root with status active.\n203: - `skill-visible`: PASS when the skill listing parses and has an entry that mentions the inspected root.\n204: - `command-listed`: PASS when every `.md` file under the project's `.opencode/commands/` folder (or its singular `command/` form) is listed under the name it registers, which is its relative path without `.md`, or on OpenCode 1 the `name:` in its leading frontmatter when it has one, and every listed name comes from a folder the runtime reads (the global config folders, and on OpenCode 2 every ancestor `.opencode` folder plus the built-ins `init` and `review`). On OpenCode 2 a `<server>:<prompt>` name also passes when `<server>` is an MCP server in the config sources the private server reports, and a PASS notes `mcp prompts: <n>`. When the config sources did not parse, a `<server>:<prompt>` name in no command folder degrades the leg instead of reading as a stray, because the server list cannot be judged. A listed name that a non-command file in the project's command folder could produce also fails, even when a global or inherited folder holds a command of that name: any file not ending in `.md`, such as `README.markdown`, `.txt` or `.yaml`, counts under its relative path both with and without its extension. The two OpenCode 2 built-ins `init` and `review` and a `<server>:<prompt>` name from a configured MCP server are exempt, because the rule catches an OpenCode version that starts scanning more than `*.md`. A name that a `.md` command in the same folder already owns is left out, because the command answers to that name.\n205: \n206: A family that is not installed is SKIP, never FAIL.\n207: \n208: On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3 cuts piped stdout at 65,536 bytes, which is why the collectors read stdout from files.\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## 6. MUTATION BOUNDARIES\n213: \n214: | Script | Boundary |\n215: |---|---|\n216: | `route-validate.sh` | Read-only during normal validation. Its self-test creates temporary fixture files and removes them on exit. |\n217: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Read-only diagnostic. Repairs belong to the `/doctor:mcp` workflows, one approval at a time. Exit 3 means bad arguments. |\n218: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Read-only contract scan. |\n219: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only audit. |\n220: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Read-only report. |\n221: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only, including the hub gap report: every validator and every hub checker runs in its check-only form and its derived-block probe is a dry run. |\n222: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | `plan` and `preserve` are read-only. `apply` is a dry run until `--apply`. An in-place conversion rewrites the skill's `SKILL.md` and creates its sibling `ROUTER.md`, and a plain skill is staged as a new root under `.skilled/skills/<name>/`. Both back the pre-write bytes up under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/` and never replace a file that exists. The plain path keeps its three temporary folders in one scratch root beside them at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, and deletes it when a question is declined and again once a finished run has reported. The integration rows then write the advisor graph, the compiled `skill-graph.json` and the regression cases file, and every one of them is listed in the undo block the run prints. |\n223: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Read-only audit. |\n224: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Read-only reports. |\n225: | `release-update.cjs` | `check` and `align --dry-run` are read-only. `align` writes a run directory. `apply`, `rollback` and `record-base` write framework files and release records under a lock. The `unlock` subcommand removes a lock only when its owner process is gone. |\n226: \n227: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | `list` is read-only. `set` is a dry run until `--apply`, which writes only `speckit.hooks.<key>` in local or global git config. |\n228: | `git-standards.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only. `init`, `set` and `disable` are dry runs until `--apply`, which writes only `.sk-git/` or the `skgit.contractDir` directory, never the shipped sk-git templates. |\n229: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Read-only. It writes no file and no git config value, and calls no allocator or `--apply` mode. |\n230: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only; `status` starts the runner's `--explain` once per batch of up to 500 sampled paths and no configured check. `init` is a dry run until `--apply`, which creates only `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` and refuses with `CONFIG_EXISTS` when that file exists. |\n231: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root: it reads the instruction file, its runtime copies and `REPO RULES.md`, runs three owner tools as children in their check-only forms and writes nothing. |\n232: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n233: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n234: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It reads `.codex/hooks.json` and the Codex config, prints no hash and takes no write path. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n235: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime's entry runs its hook script, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. Its Vibe reading is line by line, so a `.vibe/hooks.toml` that Vibe cannot parse can still read `ok vibe`, and a value with a trailing comment or a single-quoted string reads `hook-missing`. |\n236: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n237: \n238: Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 7. VALIDATION AND INSTRUCTIONS\n243: \n244: Run every doctor test from the repository root. CI runs the same command in the `doctor-scripts` job of `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml`:\n245: \n246: ```bash\n247: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/run-all.sh\n248: ```\n249: \n250: The runner needs `node`, `python3` with PyYAML, and the advisor runtime's dependencies (`npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime ci`). It exits `0` when every suite passes, `1` when one fails and `2` when a required tool is missing. `tests/README.markdown` lists the suites.\n251: \n252: Each script can be pointed at a fixture tree, which is how the tests run without touching the repository:\n253: \n254: | Script | Override |\n255: |---|---|\n256: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs`, `mcp-doctor.sh`, `release-update.cjs` | `--root <dir>` |\n257: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | `--root <dir>`. It also takes `--manifest <path>` and `--source` |\n258: | `git-hook-gates.cjs`, `git-standards.cjs`, `git-setup-check.cjs`, `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `--repo <dir>`. `git-hook-gates.cjs` also reads `GATE_REGISTRY`, and `git-setup-check.cjs` also takes `--tool <name>` |\n259: | `audit_descriptions.py`, `route-validate.sh` | `--repo-root <dir>`. `route-validate.sh` also reads `REPO_ROOT`, `ROUTES_FILE`, `DOCTOR_DIR` and `ASSETS_DIR` |\n260: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | the repository root as its first argument |\n261: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_COMMANDS_DIR` for the commands tree. `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0` reports advisory findings as warnings, and hard invariants still fail |\n262: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | `SKILL_GRAPH_FRESHNESS_ROOT`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` |\n263: \n264: ---\n265: \n266: ## 8. RELATED\n267: \n268: - [Doctor route manifest](../../../commands/doctor/_routes.yaml)\n269: - [Doctor command routers](../../../commands/doctor/)\n270: - [Doctor assets](../../../commands/doctor/assets/)\n271: - [Doctor script tests](./tests/README.markdown)\n272: - [Commands directory](../../../commands/)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown, lines 1-272\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\n3: description: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"doctor command scripts\"\n6:   - \"doctor route validation\"\n7:   - \"mcp diagnostics\"\n8: importance_tier: \"important\"\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Doctor Command Scripts\n12: \n13: > Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n14: > OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n15: >\n16: > Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n21: \n22: `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n23: \n24: The scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n29: \n30: ```text\n31: .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n32: +-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n33: +-- agents-md-check.cjs\n34: +-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n35: +-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n36: +-- audit_descriptions.py\n37: +-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n38: +-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n39: +-- ci-report.cjs\n40: +-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n41: +-- ci-toggle.cjs\n42: +-- codex-hook-approval-check.cjs\n43: +-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n44: +-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n45: +-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n46: +-- git-setup-check.cjs\n47: +-- git-standards.cjs\n48: +-- hook-wiring-check.cjs\n49: +-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n50: +-- mcp-doctor.sh\n51: +-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n52: +-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n53: +-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n54: +-- route-validate.py\n55: +-- route-validate.sh\n56: +-- release-update.cjs\n57: +-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n58: +-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n59: +-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n60: +-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n61: +-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n62: +-- lib/\n63: |   +-- agents-md-sections.cjs      # the one segmenter, classifier, block reader and delivery measurement the two engines and their suites share\n64: |   +-- ci-profile.cjs             # the repository CI profile loader the report, scaffold and toggle scripts share\n65: |   +-- ci-report-render.cjs       # the report document, its JSON form and its fixed-width table\n66: |   +-- ci-report-sources.cjs      # repository variables, the branch runs come from and the newest non-rebuild commit\n67: |   +-- ci-report-text.cjs         # the one-line rule a gh failure and a YAML parse error both need\n68: |   +-- ci-report-workflows.cjs    # workflow discovery, the YAML parse, switch states and drift flags\n69: |   +-- quality-applied-rules.cjs  # the checklists and override rows the gate would apply, asked through --explain\n70: |   +-- quality-config-init.cjs    # the one mode that writes, from the shipped template with --apply\n71: |   +-- quality-config-source.cjs  # the config source, its trust state and the hygiene rules it switches off\n72: |   +-- quality-report-view.cjs    # the read-only status and check modes\n73: |   +-- quality-script-io.cjs      # the request error and the one place a report reaches stdout\n74: |   +-- quality-skill-paths.cjs    # the one sk-code quality folder and the handles every mode reads\n75: |   +-- sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs # the pure frontmatter, router-move and legacy-router text transforms\n76: |   `-- sk-skill-surface.cjs       # shared probes the check engine and the converter both call: locate, classify, collisions, spawn\n77: +-- tests/            # one suite per script, plus run-all.sh\n78: |   `-- opencode-version-battery.test.cjs\n79: `-- README.markdown\n80: ```\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 3. KEY FILES\n85: \n86: | File | Responsibility |\n87: |---|---|\n88: | `route-validate.sh` | Runs route-manifest validation and fixture-based self-tests. |\n89: | `route-validate.py` | Implements route schema, asset, tool, mutation and display-parity checks. |\n90: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnoses supported MCP servers and runtime configuration wiring. |\n91: | `mcp-doctor-lib.sh` | Supplies logging, result tracking, JSON output and configuration helpers to `mcp-doctor.sh`. |\n92: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Enforces read-only and mutating classifications for MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n93: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Audits parent skill hubs against structural and routing invariants. |\n94: | `release-update.cjs` | Plans, aligns, applies, rolls back and records framework release bases for the five `/doctor:update` workflows. |\n95: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Compares compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph representations without writing. |\n96: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | The `/doctor:sk-skill check` engine. With no argument it prints one row per direct child of `.skilled/skills`, and with a skill name or a path one line per row `C1` to `C13`, each carrying its own status and detail. A skill inside another repository's `.skilled/skills` is read against that repository, and each of its rows names the repository it read. A tool that repository does not ship runs from the checker's own copy, and the row names that copy too. It calls the format gate, the root-metadata gate, the derived-block regeneration in dry-run form, the router-equivalence replay, the advisor graph and mirror checks and the description audit, and writes nothing. `--into-hub <hub>` prints the eleven surfaces a skill or mode must land on under that hub instead, each with the probe it ran, and still writes nothing. `--json` prints either report and nothing else. It exits 0 when no row is a finding, 1 when one is, and 2 when a precondition it needs is missing, with the provision command on its own line. |\n97: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | Plans, and on request applies, the conversion of one skill. `plan` reads, refuses and prints every step, `apply` prints the same plan and writes only with `--apply`, and `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves a reshaped body kept every original line. An older Skilled skill converts in place, with its machine router moved verbatim into a new sibling `ROUTER.md`. A plain skill reached through a path outside the skills root is staged under its frontmatter name and becomes a class S standalone root, and it converts only once the operator approves at least eight intent phrases, which become its `intent_signals`. A skill of neither shape is out of scope. Preflight refuses a hub declaration, a nested packet tree, a `local-` or `agent-` prefixed name, a name collision and a description over 130 characters. A skill that declares no `allowed-tools` field prints `allowed-tools: approval required; propose [...]` in its plan, and the apply writes no grant until a list is passed. A field declared twice is refused as `ALLOWED_TOOLS_DUPLICATE` rather than merged. The plain path keeps its agent output, its preservation snapshot and its staging copy in one scratch root beside the backup at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, deleted when a question is declined and once the run has reported. A skill whose router already lives in `ROUTER.md` gets its frontmatter normalized and nothing else. Every write is preceded by a backup under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/`, `ROUTER.md` is created exclusively, and a parsed router that would change restores the backup. After both format gates it runs the integration rows: the advisor scan, the compiled skill graph, the reach probe with the budget row, the regression cases and the changelog file and link. |\n98: | `sk-code-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the sk-code setup battery from a repository root: one verdict per setup leg, one repair command per failing leg and no writes. Its quality leg runs the quality runner's own tests, which build throwaway repositories under the OS temp folder and remove them. |\n99: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | The quality config doctor's entry, kept thin: the arguments, the run context, the one call that starts the runner, the mode table and the exit status are here, and each mode sits in the quality modules under `lib/`. It reports which quality config the sk-code quality gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, and the trust state the runner applies to that config: `trusted`, `untrusted` or `none`. `status` and `check` both print it, and when the config is untrusted they print `git config --local skilled.trustRepoChecks true` as the repair. It prints the hygiene layers, a `Hygiene:` line naming the core rules `commentHygiene.disableRules` switches off and an applied-rules block built by calling `quality-gate.cjs --explain` over a sample of one file per first three folders and extension: each checklist with its seat, its override rows or the reasons its section was refused, each same-level conflict and a `Hygiene rules:` line for the rules in force. A refused section, an unknown rule id or a missing checklist fails the status with `CONFIG_INVALID` or `CHECKLIST_MISSING`, and a conflict is listed without failing it. It validates that config and creates `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once from the shipped template for `/doctor:sk-code quality`, and in a repository with no `specs/` folder its plan suggests the spec-kit hygiene rules without writing them. It never runs a command the config names. It exits 0 on success, 1 on an invalid or missing config, a refused checklist section or a refused init and 2 on a usage error or outside a git repository. |\n100: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Audits description lengths across skills, commands and agents. |\n101: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Verifies every canonical agent reaches all five runtime surfaces, and that mirror surfaces stay symlinked rather than forked. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n102: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | The read-only AGENTS.md check: twelve rows reporting the install state, the sections lost or reordered, the currency of each body, every pin, the delivery budget, the shape of the repository block, the repository's own content and its local edits, each runtime copy, the `REPO RULES.md` router and the source template's purity. Four of them run their owner as a child instead of re-deriving it, carrying the rule-copy canary's budget lines, the gate 1 pointer sync's Cursor block, the repo-rules checker's summary and the manifest generator's check of the source template's purity. `--root <dir>` classifies another repository and `--json` prints the same report for a machine. A row it cannot prove reads `UNKNOWN` rather than `PASS`, so it exits 0 on no finding, 1 on a finding or an unproven row and 2 on a failed precondition. |\n103: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | The writing half of `/doctor:agents-md`, in three subcommands. `plan` composes every target in memory and prints the targets, the diffs, the new block, the byte budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest, and writes nothing. `apply --plan-digest <digest>` recomposes, refuses a digest the tree no longer produces, backs up every path it will touch, writes only inside the allowlist it checks in code and then proves the composition against the bytes on disk, restoring from the backup and ending `STATUS=FAIL` when one line went missing or one appeared outside the block that no shipped section carries. It refuses a symlinked `REPO RULES.md` or Cursor mirror it would write, because a write through the link would change a file the repository does not own. `restore --run=<dir>` puts back exactly the paths that run's `manifest.json` records, removes the files, and the empty folders, that run created, and keeps the backup. It exits 0 when there was nothing to do or the work finished, 1 on a failed proof or a declined run and 2 on a failed precondition or a path outside the allowlist. It exits 3 when a write fails after the backup, having restored from it or said why not. |\n104: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | Generates and gates the shipped manifest's `derived` key: a copy of every shipped body, its hash and the router rows, taken from the root documents. `--write` refreshes it and `--check` is the freshness gate, so a body that drifted from the template fails here instead of surfacing as a check finding. It refuses a heading no authored entry matches, an authored entry that matches nothing, and text above the first heading, and `--write` runs only in the repository that ships the template. Each of the three engines has its own suite: `agents-md-check.test.cjs`, `agents-md-embed.test.cjs` and `agents-md-manifest.test.cjs`, driven from the recipe folders under `tests/fixtures/agents-md/`. |\n105: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | Lists the shipped hook gates from `scripts/git-hooks/lib/gates.tsv` with their saved `speckit.hooks.<key>` values, and sets or unsets one in local or global git config for `/doctor:git hooks`. Refuses the per-push approvals. |\n106: | `git-standards.cjs` | Shows where a repository's commit, PR and branch rules come from, copies the shipped sk-git templates into `.sk-git/`, and changes a rules block setting or removes a kind's rules section in those copies for `/doctor:git standards`, re-validating each change with sk-git's own contract check. |\n107: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Runs the read-only sk-git setup battery for `/doctor:git setup`: eleven checks, each PASS, FAIL, SKIP, UNKNOWN or DEGRADED with the source it read and one repair per failure. It exits 0 when every check passes or skips, 3 when none failed but a check is UNKNOWN or DEGRADED, 1 on any failure and 2 on a usage error. It loads every helper from its own install root and never runs code from the repository it inspects. |\n108: | `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Compares every command catalog and hub metadata entry back to the command's own frontmatter, which is the one copy nothing else derives from. Structural drift fails. Prose divergence reports and fails only under `--strict`. `--check` is accepted and ignored, because it is read-only either way. |\n109: | `doctor-helper-match.cjs` | Ranks the doctor commands of `_routes.yaml` against a problem stated in plain words for `/doctor:helper`: one entry per route, standalone command, standalone action and mcp sub-action, scored by inverse-document-frequency overlap over the manifest phrases plus a bonus for a fully covered phrase, with a preference for the mutation class the operator asked for. Each candidate carries the manifest phrase that best matched and the terms it matched, so the reason on every line is evidence. It skips its own route and every private name, reads one YAML file and writes nothing. It exits 0 on a match, 1 on no candidate above the floor and 2 on a bad argument, an unreadable manifest or a missing YAML parser. |\n110: | `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` | Scores every activated doctor phrase and each runnable doctor command through the built advisor scorer, then keeps the three results the hooks show. It fails when a phrase reaches neither the top pick nor the second line. It also fails if a runnable command does not rank first or an activated phrase has fewer than three words. It exits 2 when the advisor build is missing or stale. |\n111: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | The read-only Codex approval check: it finds the advisor's `UserPromptSubmit` entry in `.codex/hooks.json` and reports whether the Codex config holds a `trusted_hash` for that exact group and index, because Codex skips an unapproved entry without a warning. `--root <dir>` reads another checkout, `--config <path>` reads another config and `--json` prints one object. It prints `SKIP no-codex-hooks`, `PASS codex-hooks-approved`, `WARN codex-advisor-hook-absent` or `WARN codex-hooks-unapproved <root>` with the `/hooks` repair line, and never prints a hash. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n112: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | The read-only hook-wiring check: it reads `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json`, `.cursor/hooks.json`, `.devin/hooks.v1.json` and `.vibe/hooks.toml`, and reports whether the prompt hook entry is wired for each runtime folder it finds. An entry counts as wired only when one of its commands, read the way a shell reads it, starts node on that runtime's hook script, so a script named in an `echo` or a comment does not count. It reads `.vibe/hooks.toml` line by line for a `pre_agent` entry that runs the Vibe turn-start hook, because the repository installs no TOML parser. Pi, DSH and OpenCode load their prompt hook through an extension or a plugin, so the check has no row for them. `--root <dir>` is required and `--json` prints one object. It prints `ok <runtime>` or `hook-missing <runtime> <file>`, skips a runtime whose folder is absent and never writes. It exits 0 when every installed runtime is wired, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. |\n113: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Checks this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. On 1 it proves plugin discovery through `debug config` and plugin load through an import check. On 2 it starts and stops a private server with the v2 binary it selected (`$OPENCODE_BIN`, `opencode` or `opencode2`, whichever reports a v2 version) and reads plugin and skill state from it. It exits 0 when every requested leg passes, 1 on a failed leg, 2 on a usage error, a crash or an `--out` file it cannot write and 3 when a leg is degraded or skipped. |\n114: | `ci-report.cjs` | The `/doctor:ci report` entry, kept thin: the flags, the exit codes and every name `/doctor:ci toggle` reads live here, and the readings live in the `ci-report` modules under `lib/`. It reads every workflow under `.github/workflows/` and reports its switch state, triggers, path filters and drift flags for `/doctor:ci report`. A switch is any variable a job's `if:` compares against `off`, so the workflow file is its only copy, and a repository variable value is reduced to a state and never printed. The branch, the required switch, the exempt files, the README and the rebuild trailer come from the CI profile, and the JSON names the profile it read. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 when a workflow fails to parse, 2 on a bad argument and 3 when the CI profile is missing or invalid. |\n115: | `ci-scaffold.cjs` | Renders the sk-code-ci workflow or job template from the CI profile for `/doctor:ci scaffold`, and with `--protected` prints the profile's protected workflow names. It writes nothing. |\n116: | `ci-toggle.cjs` | Plans one switch change for `/doctor:ci toggle`: the allowlist, the refusals from the live ruleset, `neverOff` and the README, the no-op check, the exact `gh variable` command and the second named confirmation for a protected workflow. It never changes a variable. |\n117: | `lib/ci-profile.cjs` | Finds, validates and normalizes the repository CI profile (`--profile`, then `git config skcode.ciProfile`, then `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/repository/ci.json`, then a generic default that forces nothing), rejecting an unknown key. A profile whose `repository` field names another repository is ignored, and one left at `.sk-code/ci.json` is refused. |\n118: | `lib/ci-report-sources.cjs` | Reads what the report takes from outside the workflow files: the repository variables through `gh variable list`, the branch the repository integrates on and the newest commit on that branch that is not a trigger-index rebuild. Arguments go in as an array and never through a shell, a variable value is reduced to a state and never printed, and a read that fails is reported as unknown with its reason. |\n119: | `lib/ci-report-workflows.cjs` | Reads the workflow files themselves: discovery under `.github/workflows/`, the YAML parse, the switch state per job, the drift flags, the purpose column read from the workflows README and the switch names the report lists. A switch is recognized by its shape, so any repository's switch names are read and none is assumed. |\n120: | `lib/ci-report-render.cjs` | Builds the report document and its two renderings, the JSON a machine reads and the fixed-width table a person reads, both from the same readings. The not-checked list travels with the document, so a clean row is never read as a pass for a rule no parser checks. |\n121: | `lib/ci-report-text.cjs` | `firstLine()`, the one-line rule a `gh` failure and a YAML parse error both need, because a whole message would break the table row, the JSON field and the stderr line that carry it. |\n122: | `lib/quality-skill-paths.cjs` | The one sk-code quality folder this doctor reads, and the handles inside it that answer every mode's question. Each path resolves from the file's own location, so the doctor works from a checkout anywhere, and the runner, the config reader and the helpers load here so every mode reads one skill folder, one config schema and the one runner the gate itself starts. |\n123: | `lib/quality-script-io.cjs` | The two ways the quality doctor answers whoever started it: the typed request failure that carries the exit code and the `STATUS=` error code a route reads, and `emit()`, the single place a report reaches stdout. One function writes the last line, so a route reads one whatever the mode. |\n124: | `lib/quality-config-source.cjs` | Which repository config a mode read, whether the runner trusts it and which core hygiene rules it switches off, every answer read through the module the gate itself reads so a doctor verdict and a gate verdict cannot drift. It also builds the suggestion an init prints for a repository with no `specs/` folder. |\n125: | `lib/quality-config-init.cjs` | The one mode that can write: it shows the config it would create from the shipped template and writes it only with `--apply`, through a hard link from a temporary file, so it never replaces a config that already exists. An existing config is refused rather than merged. |\n126: | `lib/quality-report-view.cjs` | The two read-only modes. `status` reports the config, its trust, the tools it names, the hygiene layers and what the gate would apply here, and `check` stops at the config itself. Both render the lines a route prints and the document `--json` carries from one set of readings, and neither starts a configured check. |\n127: | `lib/quality-applied-rules.cjs` | Which checklists and hygiene rules the gate would apply to a repository and which override rows inside them it would honour, asked of the runner itself through `--explain` over a sampled file per folder group and extension, so the doctor keeps no second copy of checklist discovery or of the override grammar. |\n128: | `lib/sk-skill-surface.cjs` | The probes `/doctor:sk-skill` shares between its check engine and its converter: resolve a name or a path to a skill root, classify where that root sits (a direct child of `.skilled/skills`, outside it or nested in a hub), read the frontmatter fields, inspect name collisions against the reserved and persona prefixes, and wrap a spawned validator so its JSON, its exit status and its missing-tool cases read the same in both scripts. |\n129: | `lib/sk-skill-convert-transforms.cjs` | The pure text transforms the converter applies, kept free of the repository so they are unit-tested on strings: normalize the `version` and `allowed-tools` frontmatter, find the machine blocks a Smart Routing section still holds, move a legacy trailing machine router section into the sibling router template, and add the router link line. |\n130: | `orphan-process-check.cjs` | Lists the helper processes and model servers this user started that were reparented to init and are older than a minimum age (60 minutes by default), classifying each as a node test, vitest, tail monitor, grep search, executor worker, session-handover test helper, model server or git-live-follow, and reports each one's age and memory with a total. `--reap` sends `SIGTERM` after re-checking each process's identity, `--yes` confirms it, and `--json` and `--summary` print machine-readable and one-line forms. It exits 0 on a printed report, 1 on an unexpected error and 2 on a bad argument. |\n131: \n132: ---\n133: \n134: ## 4. ROUTE VALIDATION\n135: \n136: `route-validate.sh` delegates the live manifest checks to `route-validate.py`.\n137: \n138: The validator checks:\n139: \n140: - Manifest parsing and schema version\n141: - Required route fields\n142: - Unique targets\n143: - Referenced YAML assets\n144: - Mutation classes\n145: - MCP tool grants\n146: - Trigger phrases\n147: - Script path resolution\n148: - Each route names an existing doctor router\n149: - Target parity between each command's router and presentation\n150: - Read-only route mutation policy\n151: - Workflow activity coverage for every route script invocation\n152: \n153: Run it from the repository root:\n154: \n155: ```bash\n156: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh\n157: ```\n158: \n159: Run its negative fixture suite:\n160: \n161: ```bash\n162: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh --self-test\n163: ```\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. DIAGNOSTIC ENTRYPOINTS\n168: \n169: | Command | Purpose |\n170: |---|---|\n171: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs [--root <dir>] [--json]` | Report the twelve AGENTS.md rows for this repository or another one, and write nothing. |\n172: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs plan [--root <dir>] [--keep-claude-md] [--create-router]` | Print every target with its diff, the new block, the budget, the preservation ledger, the backup folder and the plan digest. Writes nothing, and prints `NOTHING TO DO` when the repository is already in its target state. |\n173: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs apply --plan-digest <digest> [--root <dir>]` | Back up, write inside the allowlist and prove the composition, only with the digest the plan printed. |\n174: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs restore --run=<dir> --yes` | Put back exactly the paths one run recorded, and remove the files, and the empty folders, that run created. |\n175: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-manifest.cjs --check` | Fail when the manifest's derived key no longer matches the shipped documents. |\n176: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh` | Diagnose all supported MCP servers. |\n177: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/mcp-doctor.sh --json` | Emit machine-readable MCP diagnostics. |\n178: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs <skill-dir>` | Audit one parent skill hub. |\n179: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Report skill graph drift. |\n180: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-check.cjs check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | Report how far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format row by row, or report a skill's gaps against a hub's eleven surfaces. |\n181: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs plan <name-or-path>` | Print the conversion plan for one skill and write nothing. A plain skill refuses until at least eight `--approved-phrase` values are passed, and a skill with no `allowed-tools` field prints the list its plan proposes. |\n182: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-skill-convert.cjs apply <name-or-path> --apply` | Write a plan already shown and approved: normalize the frontmatter and move a legacy machine router into `ROUTER.md`, or stage a plain skill as a new root. Without `--apply` it prints the plan and stops. `preserve --snapshot <dir>` proves the reshaped body first and writes nothing. |\n183: | `python3 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/audit_descriptions.py --repo-root .` | Audit description budgets. |\n184: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs` | Report agent-roster coverage drift across runtimes. |\n185: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Report command-catalog and hub-metadata drift. |\n186: | `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Check the read-only and mutating classes of MCP doctor and installer scripts. |\n187: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/release-update.cjs check --offline` | Report what a release update would change, without fetching. |\n188: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-hook-gates.cjs list` | Show every hook gate and its saved setting. |\n189: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-standards.cjs status` | Show which commit, PR and branch rules are enforced, and from where. |\n190: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/git-setup-check.cjs` | Check which sk-git hooks, rules and settings are live in this repository. |\n191: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/sk-code-quality-config.cjs status` | Show which quality config the gate reads, whether each tool it names is on PATH, the trust state the runner applies to that config, the hygiene rules it switches off and the checklists and override rows the gate would apply. |\n192: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs` | Check this repository's OpenCode plugins on OpenCode 1 and 2. |\n193: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\\|v2\\|both` | Choose the family to check. The default is `both`. |\n194: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --json` | Print the JSON summary on stdout instead of the human lines. |\n195: | `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --root <dir> --out <file>` | Inspect another repository root and write the JSON summary to the named file. |\n196: \n197: The battery runs five legs:\n198: \n199: - `family-present`: PASS when a candidate's `--version` succeeds and parses to that family.\n200: - `plugin-discovered`: PASS when OpenCode 1's `debug config` exits 0 with parseable JSON, the plugin folder holds at least one top-level `.js` file, none is a broken symlink and every one is listed.\n201: - `plugin-load`: PASS when the import check exits 0 and reports every top-level `.js` file loaded.\n202: - `plugin-active`: PASS when two consecutive reads give the same non-zero count and, on that read, every top-level `.js` file has an entry under the inspected root with status active.\n203: - `skill-visible`: PASS when the skill listing parses and has an entry that mentions the inspected root.\n204: - `command-listed`: PASS when every `.md` file under the project's `.opencode/commands/` folder (or its singular `command/` form) is listed under the name it registers, which is its relative path without `.md`, or on OpenCode 1 the `name:` in its leading frontmatter when it has one, and every listed name comes from a folder the runtime reads (the global config folders, and on OpenCode 2 every ancestor `.opencode` folder plus the built-ins `init` and `review`). On OpenCode 2 a `<server>:<prompt>` name also passes when `<server>` is an MCP server in the config sources the private server reports, and a PASS notes `mcp prompts: <n>`. When the config sources did not parse, a `<server>:<prompt>` name in no command folder degrades the leg instead of reading as a stray, because the server list cannot be judged. A listed name that a non-command file in the project's command folder could produce also fails, even when a global or inherited folder holds a command of that name: any file not ending in `.md`, such as `README.markdown`, `.txt` or `.yaml`, counts under its relative path both with and without its extension. The two OpenCode 2 built-ins `init` and `review` and a `<server>:<prompt>` name from a configured MCP server are exempt, because the rule catches an OpenCode version that starts scanning more than `*.md`. A name that a `.md` command in the same folder already owns is left out, because the command answers to that name.\n205: \n206: A family that is not installed is SKIP, never FAIL.\n207: \n208: On this repository all legs pass on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32. OpenCode 1.19.3 cuts piped stdout at 65,536 bytes, which is why the collectors read stdout from files.\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## 6. MUTATION BOUNDARIES\n213: \n214: | Script | Boundary |\n215: |---|---|\n216: | `route-validate.sh` | Read-only during normal validation. Its self-test creates temporary fixture files and removes them on exit. |\n217: | `mcp-doctor.sh` | Read-only diagnostic. Repairs belong to the `/doctor:mcp` workflows, one approval at a time. Exit 3 means bad arguments. |\n218: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | Read-only contract scan. |\n219: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only audit. |\n220: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | Read-only report. |\n221: | `sk-skill-check.cjs` | Read-only, including the hub gap report: every validator and every hub checker runs in its check-only form and its derived-block probe is a dry run. |\n222: | `sk-skill-convert.cjs` | `plan` and `preserve` are read-only. `apply` is a dry run until `--apply`. An in-place conversion rewrites the skill's `SKILL.md` and creates its sibling `ROUTER.md`, and a plain skill is staged as a new root under `.skilled/skills/<name>/`. Both back the pre-write bytes up under `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/` and never replace a file that exists. The plain path keeps its three temporary folders in one scratch root beside them at `.skilled/skills/.state/doctor-sk-skill/<name>/scratch/`, and deletes it when a question is declined and again once a finished run has reported. The integration rows then write the advisor graph, the compiled `skill-graph.json` and the regression cases file, and every one of them is listed in the undo block the run prints. |\n223: | `audit_descriptions.py` | Read-only audit. |\n224: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs` | Read-only reports. |\n225: | `release-update.cjs` | `check` and `align --dry-run` are read-only. `align` writes a run directory. `apply`, `rollback` and `record-base` write framework files and release records under a lock. The `unlock` subcommand removes a lock only when its owner process is gone. |\n226: \n227: | `git-hook-gates.cjs` | `list` is read-only. `set` is a dry run until `--apply`, which writes only `speckit.hooks.<key>` in local or global git config. |\n228: | `git-standards.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only. `init`, `set` and `disable` are dry runs until `--apply`, which writes only `.sk-git/` or the `skgit.contractDir` directory, never the shipped sk-git templates. |\n229: | `git-setup-check.cjs` | Read-only. It writes no file and no git config value, and calls no allocator or `--apply` mode. |\n230: | `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `status` and `check` are read-only; `status` starts the runner's `--explain` once per batch of up to 500 sampled paths and no configured check. `init` is a dry run until `--apply`, which creates only `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` and refuses with `CONFIG_EXISTS` when that file exists. |\n231: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root: it reads the instruction file, its runtime copies and `REPO RULES.md`, runs three owner tools as children in their check-only forms and writes nothing. |\n232: | `agents-md-embed.cjs` | `plan` is read-only. `apply` writes only the paths in its own allowlist, all of them under the target root, after backing each one up under `.skilled/output/doctor-agents-md/<digest>/` (or the OS temp folder when that path is not ignored), and restores from that backup itself when its post-write proof fails. `restore` writes only the paths the run's `manifest.json` records and keeps the backup folder. It never runs `git add`, `git commit` or `git stash`. |\n233: | `agents-md-manifest.cjs` | `--check` is read-only. `--write` rewrites the manifest's `derived` key and refuses to run outside the repository that ships the template. |\n234: | `codex-hook-approval-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It reads `.codex/hooks.json` and the Codex config, prints no hash and takes no write path. It exits 0 on a skip or an approval, 1 on a missing or unapproved entry and 2 on a bad argument. |\n235: | `hook-wiring-check.cjs` | Read-only over any root. It writes no file and runs no installer, and its JSON form reports the same readings as its lines. It exits 0 when every installed runtime's entry runs its hook script, 1 when one is missing and 2 on a bad argument or a missing root. Its Vibe reading is line by line, so a `.vibe/hooks.toml` that Vibe cannot parse can still read `ok vibe`, and a value with a trailing comment or a single-quoted string reads `hook-missing`. |\n236: | `opencode-version-battery.cjs` | The battery itself writes only the `--out` file and temporary folders it removes when each step ends. The private server's folder is also removed on `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`, and the server is stopped. The import check runs each plugin's v1 factory in a child process rooted at the repository, so anything a plugin does when it loads happens during the check. It never touches the shared v2 service and never prints the private server's password. OpenCode 1 writes into a temporary data folder, not the user's. |\n237: \n238: Do not invoke a mutating path from a route classified as read-only.\n239: \n240: ---\n241: \n242: ## 7. VALIDATION AND INSTRUCTIONS\n243: \n244: Run every doctor test from the repository root. CI runs the same command in the `doctor-scripts` job of `.github/workflows/spec-kit-check.yml`:\n245: \n246: ```bash\n247: bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/run-all.sh\n248: ```\n249: \n250: The runner needs `node`, `python3` with PyYAML, and the advisor runtime's dependencies (`npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime ci`). It exits `0` when every suite passes, `1` when one fails and `2` when a required tool is missing. `tests/README.markdown` lists the suites.\n251: \n252: Each script can be pointed at a fixture tree, which is how the tests run without touching the repository:\n253: \n254: | Script | Override |\n255: |---|---|\n256: | `agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs`, `command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs`, `mcp-doctor.sh`, `release-update.cjs` | `--root <dir>` |\n257: | `agents-md-check.cjs` | `--root <dir>`. It also takes `--manifest <path>` and `--source` |\n258: | `git-hook-gates.cjs`, `git-standards.cjs`, `git-setup-check.cjs`, `sk-code-quality-config.cjs` | `--repo <dir>`. `git-hook-gates.cjs` also reads `GATE_REGISTRY`, and `git-setup-check.cjs` also takes `--tool <name>` |\n259: | `audit_descriptions.py`, `route-validate.sh` | `--repo-root <dir>`. `route-validate.sh` also reads `REPO_ROOT`, `ROUTES_FILE`, `DOCTOR_DIR` and `ASSETS_DIR` |\n260: | `check-mcp-mutation-class.sh` | the repository root as its first argument |\n261: | `parent-skill-check.cjs` | `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_COMMANDS_DIR` for the commands tree. `PARENT_HUB_CHECK_STRICT=0` reports advisory findings as warnings, and hard invariants still fail |\n262: | `skill-graph-freshness.cjs` | `SKILL_GRAPH_FRESHNESS_ROOT`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR` |\n263: \n264: ---\n265: \n266: ## 8. RELATED\n267: \n268: - [Doctor route manifest](../../../commands/doctor/_routes.yaml)\n269: - [Doctor command routers](../../../commands/doctor/)\n270: - [Doctor assets](../../../commands/doctor/assets/)\n271: - [Doctor script tests](./tests/README.markdown)\n272: - [Commands directory](../../../commands/)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760792962,"end":1794760793192}}}}
