{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794761463555,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","part":{"partID":"prt_1e01e93e0002L0j7j6t48atiWe","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","messageID":"msg_1e01e800600117ZDrotLourjcO","type":"tool","id":"call_36f21e38027c415e9a71c3f0","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"REQ-0\" specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/029-direct-call-kill-switch/spec.md | head -20; echo \"=== 028\"; grep -n \"REQ-0\" specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/028-direct-call-off-switch/spec.md | head -20"},"output":"176:| REQ-001 | Whenever `hookFlagDecision('skill-advisor')` says disabled in the caller's environment and flags file, `advisor_recommend` through the CLI client exits 0 and prints `status: ok` with `recommendations: []`, `freshness: unavailable`, `warnings: [\"ADVISOR_DISABLED\"]` and `abstainReasons: [\"Skill advisor disabled by <key>.\"]`, where `<key>` is the key the resolver returned, sends no request to a daemon that would answer and starts no launcher. |\n177:| REQ-002 | Every line of the reproduction prints `match=yes`, so the direct call abstains exactly when the hooks are off, with the same key, and answers when the environment's `0` beats the file's `1`. |\n178:| REQ-003 | With no switch set, the client still asks the daemon and prints its rows. Every `hook=on` line of the reproduction prints the same `recs=` as its before line. |\n179:| REQ-004 | The module holds no copy of the resolver's rules: it calls `hookFlagDecision` from `HF`, holds no truthy literal and no name list of its own, and `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS` is built from `MASTER_FLAG`, `MASTER_ALIASES`, `concernFlag('skill-advisor')` and `LEGACY_ALIASES['skill-advisor']`. The client's static closure gains no package, and `HF` and `env-aliases.cjs` require only `node:fs`, `node:path` and `./env-aliases.cjs`. |\n180:| REQ-005 | The floor lines and the eval line equal their before lines, `BUILD` and `TC` exit 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with at least as many passed as its before line plus the new cases, and `DT`, `VP` and `HFT` report `fail 0`. |\n186:| REQ-006 | The handler decides from the daemon's own environment and never reads the flags file, the launcher's allowlist forwards no switch name and no `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG`, and the launcher file is unchanged. |\n187:| REQ-007 | The compat contract is untouched: `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`, `lib/compat/` and `scripts/skill_advisor.py` show no diff, and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is still read as exactly `1` there. |\n188:| REQ-008 | No CLI or handler suite reads the real flags file or an exported switch: the isolated scope points `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG` at a path that does not exist, and the handler suite strips every name in `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS` before each case. |\n189:| REQ-009 | The hook doc says the direct call uses the adapters' resolution, the CP-003 step checks the canonical name with a truthy value, the example file's advisor line names the direct call, and every other doc line about the switch is listed in `plan.md` as unchanged or handed on, with its reason. |\n190:| REQ-010 | No code comment added by this phase names a spec path, a phase, a task, a finding or a decision id, and the phase diff touches no `AGENTS.md`, no row file, no package file, no committed baseline, no file of phase 025 and no file outside `FILES`. |\n191:| REQ-011 | One Opus reviewer reads the fix diff and reports 0 P0 and 0 P1, or each P0 and P1 it reports becomes a task that is fixed, committed and rechecked before the close-out. |\n=== 028\n181:| REQ-001 | With `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` or `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` in the caller's environment, `advisor_recommend` through the CLI client exits 0 and prints `status: ok` with `recommendations: []`, `freshness: unavailable`, `warnings: [\"ADVISOR_DISABLED\"]` and `abstainReasons: [\"Skill advisor disabled by <name>.\"]`, sends no request to a daemon that would answer, and starts no launcher. |\n182:| REQ-002 | With neither name set, the client still asks the daemon and prints its rows, and every `on` line of the reproduction equals its before line. |\n183:| REQ-003 | `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS` is defined once, in `ADV/lib/advisor-disabled.ts`, the handler and the client both decide through `disabledRecommendOutput`, and the handler's two existing disabled cases pass unchanged. |\n184:| REQ-004 | The floor lines (typed 24 of 44, false offers 0 of 35, plain holdout 1 of 44) and the eval line `[160,51,21,5]` equal their before lines, `BUILD` and `TC` exit 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed and at least as many passed as its before line plus the new cases, and the doctor tests and the OpenCode plugin test report `fail 0`. |\n185:| REQ-005 | The reproduction run after the fix prints `disabledCount=1` and the abstain reason on each of its five `off` lines, warm and fresh, flag first and plain first, and the stdin form. |\n191:| REQ-006 | The client's runtime import closure holds no native package: `import-closure.mjs` prints no `better-sqlite3` for `skill-advisor-cli.ts`, and `skill-advisor-cli.ts` imports nothing under `handlers/`. |\n192:| REQ-007 | The hook doc names the two switch names and the value `1` as what makes the direct call abstain and says it decides in the caller's process, CP-003 step 1 runs against the warm daemon and checks that an unflagged call still recommends, and the catalog page names the shared module and the client's early check. Every other doc line about the switch is listed in `plan.md` as unchanged with its reason. |\n193:| REQ-008 | The isolated CLI test scope strips every name in `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS`, so a switch exported in the coordinator's shell does not turn the CLI suites red. |\n194:| REQ-009 | No code comment added by this phase names a spec path, a phase, a task, a finding or a decision id, and the phase diff touches no `AGENTS.md`, no row file, no package file, no committed baseline and no file outside `FILES`. |\n195:| REQ-010 | One Opus reviewer reads the fix diff and reports 0 P0 and 0 P1, or each P0 and P1 it reports becomes a task that is fixed, committed and rechecked before the close-out. |\n248:- **NFR-R01**: The client's runtime import closure gains the new module, the schema module and `zod`, and no native package (REQ-006).\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"176:| REQ-001 | Whenever `hookFlagDecision('skill-advisor')` says disabled in the caller's environment and flags file, `advisor_recommend` through the CLI client exits 0 and prints `status: ok` with `recommendations: []`, `freshness: unavailable`, `warnings: [\"ADVISOR_DISABLED\"]` and `abstainReasons: [\"Skill advisor disabled by <key>.\"]`, where `<key>` is the key the resolver returned, sends no request to a daemon that would answer and starts no launcher. |\n177:| REQ-002 | Every line of the reproduction prints `match=yes`, so the direct call abstains exactly when the hooks are off, with the same key, and answers when the environment's `0` beats the file's `1`. |\n178:| REQ-003 | With no switch set, the client still asks the daemon and prints its rows. Every `hook=on` line of the reproduction prints the same `recs=` as its before line. |\n179:| REQ-004 | The module holds no copy of the resolver's rules: it calls `hookFlagDecision` from `HF`, holds no truthy literal and no name list of its own, and `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS` is built from `MASTER_FLAG`, `MASTER_ALIASES`, `concernFlag('skill-advisor')` and `LEGACY_ALIASES['skill-advisor']`. The client's static closure gains no package, and `HF` and `env-aliases.cjs` require only `node:fs`, `node:path` and `./env-aliases.cjs`. |\n180:| REQ-005 | The floor lines and the eval line equal their before lines, `BUILD` and `TC` exit 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with at least as many passed as its before line plus the new cases, and `DT`, `VP` and `HFT` report `fail 0`. |\n186:| REQ-006 | The handler decides from the daemon's own environment and never reads the flags file, the launcher's allowlist forwards no switch name and no `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG`, and the launcher file is unchanged. |\n187:| REQ-007 | The compat contract is untouched: `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`, `lib/compat/` and `scripts/skill_advisor.py` show no diff, and `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` is still read as exactly `1` there. |\n188:| REQ-008 | No CLI or handler suite reads the real flags file or an exported switch: the isolated scope points `HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG` at a path that does not exist, and the handler suite strips every name in `ADVISOR_DISABLED_FLAGS` before each case. |\n189:| REQ-009 | The hook doc says the direct call uses the adapters' resolution, the CP-003 step checks the canonical name with a truthy value, the example file's advisor line names the direct call, and every other doc line about the switch is listed in `plan.md` as unchanged or handed on, with its reason. |\n190:| REQ-010 | No code comment added by this phase names a spec path, a phase, a task, a finding or a decision id, and the phase diff touches no `AGENTS.md`, no row file, no package file, no committed baseline, no file of phase 025 and no file outside `FILES`. |\n191:| REQ-011 | One Opus reviewer reads the fix diff and reports 0 P0 and 0 P1, or each P0 and P1 it reports becomes a task that is fixed, committed and rechecked before the close-out. |\n=== 028\n181:| REQ-001 | With `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` or `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` in the caller's environment, `advisor_recommend` through the CLI client exits 0 and prints `status: ok` with `recommendations: []`, `freshness: unavailable`, `warnings: [\"ADVISOR_DISABLED\"]` and `abstainReasons: [\"Skill advisor disabled by <name>.\"]`, sends no request to a daemon that would answer, and starts no launcher. |\n182:| R