{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794761961064,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0262134002lrRKdV1WfjaaUE","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd5ffeHQJCqAmpoKpkZG","messageID":"msg_1e0260bdf001F1Xfe6Hkh3Dk7K","type":"tool","id":"call_23663f8cfe3849b6bcfe3ce7","tool":"edit","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"newString":"- Reading phase 029's `AC-009` as evidence for the alias rows failed: it verifies that the listed lines did not change, not that they are true (iteration 2).\n- Reading the Vibe reader as covering every field the host acts on failed: it reads only `type` and `command`, so a `match` filter is invisible even though the repository's own hooks file uses `match` values, including `agents_spawn` (iteration 3).\n- Reading the hook contract's per-runtime section as complete after the Vibe row failed: the section intro and the \"starting a surfaced command\" table still enumerate the older runtimes only (iteration 3).\n\n---\n\n## 9C. WHAT WORKED (continued)\n\n- Running the wiring check on this checkout before judging it: five `ok` lines and exit 0 prove the real `.vibe/hooks.toml` parses through the line reader, so the finding is the ignored `match` field rather than the reader (iteration 3).\n- Counting the three Vibe script names in the workflow and the route instead of trusting the comment: nine and three lines match the contract test's assertions and phase 030's `REQ-005` (iteration 3).\n- Scanning the twelve scoped files for corpus names: no hit, which is the holdout-hygiene evidence, kept to names and counts (iteration 3).","oldString":"- Reading phase 029's `AC-009` as evidence for the alias rows failed: it verifies that the listed lines did not change, not that they are true (iteration 2).","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/vibe-hooks-doctor-5/deep-review-strategy.md"},"output":"Edited specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/r